  "text": "Train a hiring model on a company's past hires and it faithfully reproduces that company's past bias. People imagine the model holding an opinion. It does not hold any. Everything it does came out of the examples it was shown, and nothing else. If those examples were skewed, then the pattern it found is the skew. And that is worse than prejudice, because it comes back looking objective. A person can be argued with. A number on a screen gets treated as a measurement. The same thing happens with gaps. Image systems worked badly on darker skin, for years. Nothing in the system decided that. The absence did. Which means you cannot audit these things by reading the code. You have to audit the data. The model is not biased. It is an extremely accurate mirror, and that is the problem. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
